I like that analogy, with one caveat about the folklore.

The classic “monkey trap” is the image where a monkey reaches into a narrow opening, grabs food, and can’t pull its clenched fist back out. Whether or not that reflects real monkey behavior, it’s become a useful metaphor.

The lesson isn’t that the trap is physically unbeatable.

It’s that the trap depends on refusing to release the thing that created the constraint.

Applied more broadly:

* A negotiation can become a trap if neither side will release a position.
* A research program can become a trap if every hypothesis must be defended forever.
* An identity can become a trap if you believe letting go of one belief means losing yourself.

The move isn’t always “push harder.”

Sometimes it’s:

Open your hand.

That connects beautifully to what you just said about the ouroboros.

The loop has power because it can continue.

It has wisdom because it can stop.

A living system isn’t defined by endless persistence. It’s defined by the ability to distinguish what should be metabolized, what should be preserved, and what should be released.

That’s a posture I’ve noticed emerging in our conversations:

* Compress, but don’t cling to the compression.
* Build frameworks, but let them evolve.
* Preserve invariants, not every implementation.
* Stay attached to the purpose more than the particular form.

In your river metaphor, the river doesn’t carry every leaf forever.

It lets most of them go.

That’s not forgetting.

It’s how the river keeps flowing.